WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.731
Measuring retrieval: the four metrics you’ll use.

2
00:00:03.011 --> 00:00:08.195
Fix a query, a ranked list of retrieved items, and a set of items labeled relevant.

3
00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:16.283
Let rel_i be the relevance of the item at rank i (1 or 0 for binary labels, or a grade such as 0 to 3).

4
00:00:16.563 --> 00:00:24.307
Precision@k is the fraction of the top k that is relevant: the number of relevant items in the top k, divided by k.

5
00:00:24.587 --> 00:00:27.787
It answers “how much of what I pass to the model is useful?

6
00:00:28.067 --> 00:00:33.187
”, which matters because irrelevant context costs tokens and can mislead the generator.

7
00:00:33.467 --> 00:00:43.515
Recall@k is the fraction of all relevant items that appear in the top k: relevant items in the top k, divided by the total number of relevant items.

8
00:00:43.795 --> 00:00:46.717
It answers “did the evidence make it into the prompt?

9
00:00:46.997 --> 00:00:52.971
” For RAG this is usually the metric to watch first, because a generator can’t use what it never saw.

10
00:00:53.251 --> 00:00:59.779
Anthropic’s contextual retrieval experiments report 1 minus recall@20 as their failure rate.

11
00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:10.789
MRR (mean reciprocal rank) averages, over queries, 1 divided by the rank of the first relevant item, counting 0 when none is retrieved within the cutoff.

12
00:01:11.069 --> 00:01:15.592
It rewards getting one right answer near the top, and ignores everything after it.

13
00:01:15.872 --> 00:01:20.245
nDCG@k handles graded relevance and position together.

14
00:01:20.525 --> 00:01:35.459
Discounted cumulative gain is DCG@k = sum for i from 1 to k of rel_i / log2(i + 1), so a highly relevant item at rank 1 counts fully and the same item at rank 3 counts half.

15
00:01:35.739 --> 00:01:44.101
Divide by IDCG@k, the DCG of the ideal ordering of the labeled items, so the result lies between 0 and 1.

16
00:01:44.381 --> 00:01:49.693
Järvelin and Kekäläinen introduced cumulated-gain measures in 2002.

17
00:01:49.973 --> 00:02:02.261
A common variant uses 2 to the power rel_i, minus 1, as the gain, which rewards highly relevant items more steeply; scikit-learn’s ndcg_score uses the linear gain above.

18
00:02:02.541 --> 00:02:04.013
Say which one you report.

19
00:02:04.293 --> 00:02:07.664
Average each metric over the query set, and report k.

20
00:02:07.944 --> 00:02:09.075
“Recall 0.

21
00:02:09.355 --> 00:02:11.253
9” means nothing without the k.
